WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:01.667
MLflow.

2
00:00:01.967 --> 00:00:07.433
Tracking, registry, and packaging in one self-hostable tool, with no vendor required.

3
00:00:08.633 --> 00:00:11.367
MLflow bundles three lifecycle stations.

4
00:00:11.667 --> 00:00:17.333
At the bottom, tracking: runs, parameters, metrics, artifacts, and the comparison UI.

5
00:00:17.633 --> 00:00:22.233
Above it, the registry: versions, aliases, and lineage back to runs.

6
00:00:22.533 --> 00:00:25.879
And on top, models: a packaging format called pyfunc,

7
00:00:25.879 --> 00:00:31.433
which makes any model loadable and servable the same way, whatever framework trained it.

8
00:00:32.633 --> 00:00:34.200
Here is how it fits together.

9
00:00:34.500 --> 00:00:36.500
Training scripts log into the server.

10
00:00:36.800 --> 00:00:40.800
The server is just a Python package, a UI, and a database.

11
00:00:41.100 --> 00:00:43.400
Serving then loads models back out of it.

12
00:00:44.600 --> 00:00:47.367
Before the code, here is what autolog supports.

13
00:00:47.667 --> 00:00:48.700
Scikit-learn.

14
00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:49.867
PyTorch.

15
00:00:50.167 --> 00:00:51.367
XGBoost.

16
00:00:51.667 --> 00:00:52.539
And LightGBM.

17
00:00:52.539 --> 00:00:57.167
It is the lowest-friction on-ramp to tracked experiments that exists.

18
00:00:58.367 --> 00:01:00.500
Its killer convenience is autolog.

19
00:01:00.800 --> 00:01:05.467
One line instruments sklearn, PyTorch, XGBoost and friends.

20
00:01:05.767 --> 00:01:07.867
The training code itself does not change.

21
00:01:08.167 --> 00:01:10.679
Even the score is logged automatically.

22
00:01:10.679 --> 00:01:14.867
Run it twice with different parameters, open the UI, and compare.

23
00:01:16.067 --> 00:01:17.700
Here is a realistic setup.

24
00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:24.933
You run MLflow on a small EC2 instance, with S3 for artifacts and Postgres as the backend.

25
00:01:25.233 --> 00:01:31.167
Every project logs to it: classifier retrains, RL runs, even evaluation scores.

26
00:01:31.467 --> 00:01:37.133
When a recruiter asks how you manage experiments, you screen-share one UI with a year of evidence.

27
00:01:38.333 --> 00:01:39.194
Let's recap.

28
00:01:39.194 --> 00:01:41.633
MLflow is the open-source default.

29
00:01:41.933 --> 00:01:44.667
It covers tracking, the registry, and packaging.

30
00:01:44.967 --> 00:01:47.600
Autolog is the lowest-friction way to start.

31
00:01:47.900 --> 00:01:50.666
And it is self-hosted, so you own the data.

32
00:01:50.666 --> 00:01:52.467
Next up: Weights and Biases.
